WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.372
This table summarizes the practical choices.

00:00:02.372 --> 00:00:04.442
One benchmark suggests a scalar.

00:00:04.442 --> 00:00:08.492
Membership suggests IN. Existence suggests EXISTS.

00:00:08.492 --> 00:00:11.112
No matching row suggests NOT EXISTS.

00:00:11.112 --> 00:00:14.452
At least one and every comparison suggest ANY and ALL.

00:00:14.452 --> 00:00:17.817
A calculation that depends on the current row suggests correlation.

00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:21.372
And an aggregate-first workflow often suggests a derived table.

00:00:21.372 --> 00:00:26.182
Connect this idea to a hospital identifying patients above a benchmark or with no follow-up.

00:00:26.182 --> 00:00:30.702
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:30.702 --> 00:00:37.147
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:37.147 --> 00:00:41.572
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:41.572 --> 00:00:45.680
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
